1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,234
More people have stood on the Moon than have reached the bottom of this place.

2
00:00:04,234 --> 00:00:06,054
And it is on our own planet.

3
00:00:07,199 --> 00:00:11,797
In twenty nineteen, a man reached the deepest point on Earth.

4
00:00:11,797 --> 00:00:18,067
Seven miles down, in total darkness, he looked outside — and saw a plastic bag.

5
00:00:19,199 --> 00:00:22,984
This is a descent to the bottom of the Mariana Trench.

6
00:00:22,984 --> 00:00:28,145
We are going to fall almost eleven kilometers, and at every depth, something is waiting.

7
00:00:28,145 --> 00:00:33,305
Watch the number on the left. It does not stop until we hit the floor.

8
00:00:34,433 --> 00:00:40,493
The deepest place on Earth is scarier than space — because space is empty, and this is not.

9
00:00:41,633 --> 00:00:46,884
Zero meters. The surface. The last time you will see sunlight.

10
00:00:48,033 --> 00:00:51,220
Two hundred meters down, the sunlight begins to die.

11
00:00:51,220 --> 00:00:55,471
Below this line, there is not enough light for anything to grow.

12
00:00:55,471 --> 00:01:00,075
Every living thing beneath us survives on darkness, or makes its own light.

13
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:03,845
One thousand meters. The midnight zone.

14
00:01:03,845 --> 00:01:08,254
From here to the bottom, the sun has never reached.

15
00:01:08,254 --> 00:01:15,750
It is permanently, completely black — colder than your refrigerator, and crushing harder every second we fall.

16
00:01:16,866 --> 00:01:21,104
You have heard that this pressure would crush you like a soda can.

17
00:01:21,104 --> 00:01:24,363
That is actually wrong — and the truth is stranger.

18
00:01:25,499 --> 00:01:28,261
Pressure only crushes what holds air against it.

19
00:01:28,261 --> 00:01:36,891
The animals down here have no air pockets — their bodies are flooded, in balance with the sea, so the weight passes straight through them.

20
00:01:36,891 --> 00:01:40,688
They are not armored against the deep. They ARE the deep.

21
00:01:40,688 --> 00:01:44,830
A human, full of air, is what the ocean would fold shut.

22
00:01:45,966 --> 00:01:50,328
One thousand meters. Something the size of a bus is hunting here.

23
00:01:51,466 --> 00:01:52,558
The giant squid.

24
00:01:52,558 --> 00:01:59,110
For centuries it was a sailor's myth — until we finally filmed one alive in two thousand twelve.

25
00:01:59,110 --> 00:02:07,845
Eyes the size of dinner plates, the largest in the animal kingdom, evolved to gather the faintest glow in a world with no light.

26
00:02:08,966 --> 00:02:11,575
Two thousand nine hundred ninety two meters.

27
00:02:11,575 --> 00:02:19,774
A Cuvier's beaked whale was tracked to this depth on a single breath — the deepest dive by any mammal ever recorded.

28
00:02:19,774 --> 00:02:22,756
It held that breath for over two hours.

29
00:02:22,756 --> 00:02:25,737
Below this point, no air-breathing animal can follow.

30
00:02:26,866 --> 00:02:28,560
Three thousand eight hundred meters.

31
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:34,319
This is where the Titanic lies — a wreck we think of as impossibly deep. Remember it.

32
00:02:34,319 --> 00:02:38,046
Because we are not even a third of the way down.

33
00:02:39,166 --> 00:02:45,388
In twenty sixteen, scientists lowered a titanium hydrophone all the way to the bottom, expecting silence.

34
00:02:45,388 --> 00:02:49,277
Instead, the deepest place on Earth was full of sound.

35
00:02:49,277 --> 00:02:56,276
This is real audio recorded down there — a ship, passing on the surface almost seven miles above.

36
00:02:57,400 --> 00:03:11,400
[Hydrophone at Challenger Deep — a ship passing seven miles above]

37
00:03:12,533 --> 00:03:17,131
Six thousand meters. We now enter a place with its own name for hell.

38
00:03:18,266 --> 00:03:24,121
Six thousand meters down, we cross into the hadal zone — named for Hades, the Greek underworld.

39
00:03:24,121 --> 00:03:27,565
Only a handful of trenches on Earth reach this deep.

40
00:03:27,565 --> 00:03:31,353
Almost nothing that lives up here has ever been seen alive.

41
00:03:32,499 --> 00:03:34,237
And it is not empty.

42
00:03:34,237 --> 00:03:42,926
Down here lives a crustacean that should be the size of your fingernail — but in the crushing dark it grows to thirty four centimeters.

43
00:03:42,926 --> 00:03:44,664
Scientists call it the supergiant.

44
00:03:44,664 --> 00:03:48,486
No one fully understands why the deep makes its animals enormous.

45
00:03:49,633 --> 00:03:55,385
The hydrophone at the bottom heard something else, too — the groan of the planet itself.

46
00:03:55,385 --> 00:04:00,059
Earthquakes, thousands of them, felt as sound at the deepest point on Earth.

47
00:04:00,059 --> 00:04:03,295
This is a real recording of the seafloor shaking.

48
00:04:04,433 --> 00:04:26,433
[Hydrophone — an earthquake, recorded on the ocean floor]

49
00:04:27,566 --> 00:04:31,119
Eight thousand meters. We reach the last fish in the world.

50
00:04:32,266 --> 00:04:37,172
Eight thousand meters. The Mariana snailfish — the deepest fish ever caught.

51
00:04:37,172 --> 00:04:45,349
It has no scales, no swim bladder; it is nearly translucent, and it rules the trench as its top predator.

52
00:04:45,349 --> 00:04:49,846
It looks fragile. It is the most successful thing down here.

53
00:04:50,966 --> 00:04:54,643
Eight thousand two hundred meters. And here, fish simply stop.

54
00:04:54,643 --> 00:05:01,261
Below this depth, the chemistry that keeps a fish's cells intact fails — they cannot exist any deeper.

55
00:05:01,261 --> 00:05:07,512
For the final two and a half kilometers of our descent, there are no fish at all.

56
00:05:07,512 --> 00:05:10,453
We are going somewhere even they cannot survive.

57
00:05:11,599 --> 00:05:15,308
Ten thousand meters. The machines start to die.

58
00:05:16,433 --> 00:05:19,468
At these depths, the ocean kills our machines.

59
00:05:19,468 --> 00:05:31,610
In two thousand fourteen, a deep-sea robot descending under sixteen thousand pounds of pressure per square inch simply imploded — crushed out of existence in an instant, seven hours into its dive.

60
00:05:32,733 --> 00:05:35,977
Ten thousand six hundred meters. Almost the floor.

61
00:05:35,977 --> 00:05:42,871
And clinging to it — giant single cells, each one the size of your hand. Not animals.

62
00:05:42,871 --> 00:05:49,765
Single living cells, among the largest on Earth, alive under a weight that would flatten a tank.

63
00:05:50,899 --> 00:05:58,423
In nineteen sixty, the first two men ever to reach the bottom claimed they saw a flatfish lying on the floor.

64
00:05:58,423 --> 00:06:00,214
For decades it was famous.

65
00:06:00,214 --> 00:06:07,737
Then scientists proved a fish cannot survive that deep — one of the men later admitted, we were engineers, not biologists.

66
00:06:07,737 --> 00:06:10,961
What they really saw is still argued about today.

67
00:06:12,099 --> 00:06:17,245
Ten thousand nine hundred thirty five meters. The bottom of the world.

68
00:06:18,366 --> 00:06:21,622
Ten thousand nine hundred thirty five meters. Challenger Deep.

69
00:06:21,622 --> 00:06:24,879
The deepest point on the surface of the Earth.

70
00:06:24,879 --> 00:06:32,838
The pressure here is eight tons pressing on every square inch — the weight of a hundred adults, balanced on your thumbnail.

71
00:06:33,966 --> 00:06:37,124
Fewer than thirty human beings have ever been here.

72
00:06:37,124 --> 00:06:44,493
And when they arrived, at the deepest, most remote point on our entire planet, they found something already waiting for them.

73
00:06:45,633 --> 00:06:50,946
Plastic. A bag, and a sweet wrapper, on the floor of the deepest place on Earth.

74
00:06:50,946 --> 00:07:00,575
We found chemicals banned in the nineteen seventies inside creatures at the very bottom — poison that reached the deepest point on the planet before most humans ever did.

75
00:07:01,699 --> 00:07:05,967
We have better maps of the surface of Mars than of our own ocean floor.

76
00:07:05,967 --> 00:07:09,096
The scariest place in the universe is not in the sky.

77
00:07:09,096 --> 00:07:14,786
It is under the water, on the planet you were born on — and we have barely begun to look.

78
00:07:15,933 --> 00:07:23,086
Every sound in this video was real, recorded at the bottom of the sea, and every source is in the description.

79
00:07:23,086 --> 00:07:30,580
If this trench unsettled you, wait until you hear what deep space sounds like — that video is on your screen now.

80
00:07:30,580 --> 00:07:32,965
Subscribe to mindwired, and keep going down.

